<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Ablation study of classifier types under FO optimization of the classifier and ZO updates on the adapter</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.SSx5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.SSx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.SSx5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of ZO Placement: </span>
Table <a href="#Sx1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ E. Component Analysis: Classifier Variants and FO, ZO swap ‣ Appendix ‣ More Than Memory Savings: Zeroth-Order Optimization Mitigates Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> isolates the impact of applying ZO optimization to different trainable components of the model. When the adapter is optimized with ZO and the classifier remains FO, that is, ZO-FC, the model achieves strong performance. In contrast, reversing this setup and applying ZO to the classifier while keeping the adapter FO optimized results in remarkable performance degradation. This contrast confirms that the classifier requires precise gradient-based optimization to maintain stable decision boundaries across tasks, while the adapter can tolerate noisy ZO updates without compromising overall performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx1.T6.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx1.T6.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx1.T6.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="Sx1.T6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="Sx1.T6.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="Sx1.T6.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR100 Inc-5</span></td>
